C={
761:('the abbot’s room',['the abbatial room'],['abbot room','abbot chamber','Vimalakirti room','teacher private room'],'The abbot’s room is the monastery room occupied by the presiding teacher and used for receiving visitors.','Sixin Wuxin’s visitor enters that room to ask about the teaching, while Tiantai Deshao’s scene measures it against Vimalakirti’s famously capacious chamber. The term still denotes an actual monastery room: neither comparison licenses translating every occurrence as a mystical inner space.'),
762:('the monastery gate',['the main gate'],['monastery gate','temple gate','main gate','three gates'],'The monastery gate is the formal entrance to the monastic compound.','Zhenjing Kewen makes the gate part of a challenge at the moment of arrival, and Guangsheng Shihu’s record tests what can be said at that threshold. Monastic rules also use it prosaically as an architectural and administrative boundary, so the charged encounter scenes do not erase the ordinary gate.'),
763:('a thousand buddhas',['the thousand buddhas'],['thousand buddhas','one thousand buddhas','buddhas of the fortunate aeon','thousand-Buddha assembly'],'A thousand buddhas is the large inherited company invoked as past, present, or future awakened figures.','Baohua Xian places this immense company against the response demanded in his own hall; Yuansou Xingduan invokes it in an imperial-birthday assembly, and Yongming Yanshou treats it as a canonical multitude. These scenes establish a collective, not one person named “Thousand Buddhas.”'),
764:('now say: ultimately, how is it?',['tell me: in the end, how is it?'],['ultimately how is it','in the end how','tell me ultimately','after all how is it'],'“Now say: ultimately, how is it?” demands the final handling of the matter just raised.','Wuzu Fayan places the demand after his preceding setup, and Touzi Yiqing uses it to force the assembly beyond what has already been said. Because the answers differ from case to case, the formula marks the handoff to a final response; it is not itself a coded answer.'),
765:('the canon librarian',['scripture-store officer'],['canon librarian','scripture librarian','canon-store master','library officer'],'The canon librarian is the monastic officer responsible for the scripture store and its collection.','Liao’an Qingyu and Chushi Fanqi address identifiable holders of this office within working monasteries, while Gulin Qingmao’s documentary material places it among appointed posts. The title names a person responsible for the canon store, not the canon itself and not every monk who reads scripture.'),
766:('the eighth day of the twelfth month',['Buddha-awakening day'],['eighth of the twelfth month','laba','Buddha awakening day','December eighth observance'],'The eighth day of the twelfth lunar month is the calendrical observance associated with Shakyamuni’s awakening.','Chijue Daochong speaks on the date, Xueyan Zuqin turns its awakening story into a challenge under the morning star, and Feiyin Tongrong marks it in a formal sermon. The calendar sense remains primary: not every occurrence asserts that the historical event literally happened again.'),
767:('the first phrase',['the opening phrase'],['first phrase','opening phrase','first saying','first line'],'The first phrase is the opening verbal turn singled out for examination or contrasted with later phrases.','Yuanwu Keqin asks what belongs in that first position, and Linji Yixuan’s material ranks phrases in a larger verbal structure. Since Muchen Conglang and Hanyue Fazang supply different surrounding formulations, “first” identifies a slot in an exchange, not one fixed sentence shared by every lineage.'),
768:('the crown of the head',['the very top of the head'],['crown of the head','top of the head','vertex','overhead'],'The crown of the head is the highest point of a person’s head.','Tianyi Yihuai asks after the eye located there; Zhenjing Kewen and Yuanwu Keqin put blows or decisive action at the same bodily summit. The added eye and blows are distinct constructions around one location, so they do not turn the bare word into “insight” or “authority.”'),
769:('a dragon king',['the dragon king'],['dragon king','naga king','rain dragon king','sea dragon ruler'],'A dragon king is a powerful nāga ruler associated with water, rain, and attendance on buddhas.','Yongming Yanshou inherits the figure from scripture; Tianyi Yihuai and Sanjiao Zhisong pull him into startling public images, while the rain-invocation source addresses his weather office directly. Those roles overlap but do not prove that every dragon king in the corpus is the same named individual.'),
770:('the final move',['the last decisive move'],['final move','last move','ultimate move','final stroke'],'The final move is the last decisive action or verbal turn demanded after everything preliminary has been exhausted.','Langting Jingting asks for it after the preceding handling, while Yinyuan Longqi and Zhanran Yuancheng preserve other scenes in which something further is still owed. Their moves are not interchangeable, so the entry defines the demanded position rather than inventing one universal final stroke.'),
}
